## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design simple online statistics with fixed small memory. Mode is a practical KPI for signal quality in routing decisions on ride-hailing platforms. It requires maintaining counts and batch updates.

## Key Concepts

- Online counters on a small alphabet
- Tie-breaking and stability
- Time/memory trade-offs in streaming

## Code Example

```javascript
function OnlineMode() {
  const freq = [0,0,0,0,0,0]; // 1..5
  let mode = 1, modeCount = 0;
  return {
    update(batch){
      for (const r of batch){
        freq[r]++;
        if (freq[r] > modeCount || (freq[r] === modeCount && r < mode)){
          mode = r; modeCount = freq[r];
        }
      }
      return {mode, modeCount};
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for a larger rating scale (e.g., 1–1000)?
- How would you support sliding-window mode across the last K batches?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests an engineer's ability to design incremental statistics with tight per-batch updates and constant-time queries, a realistic analytics constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Maintaining a nonlinear statistic (entropy) via a linearizable surrogate S
- Precomputing k log k and handling zero edges cleanly
- Incremental updates using two counters per move

## Code Example
```javascript
// G[k] = k * Math.log(k), with G[0] = 0
function processBatch(batch, freq, G, S, n) {
  for (const {from,to} of batch) {
    const fFrom = freq[from];
    freq[from] = fFrom - 1;
    S.value += G[fFrom - 1] - G[fFrom];
    const fTo = freq[to];
    freq[to] = fTo + 1;
    S.value += G[fTo + 1] - G[fTo];
  }
  return Math.log(n) - S.value / n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if m changes over time or updates include new categories?
- How do you handle numerical stability for very large n and skewed distributions?

